load @term/seed/algebra/cayley-dickson
  find cayley-dickson-double

load @term/seed/algebra/normed-division
  find normed-division-algebra

# The complex numbers, the Cayley-Dickson double of the reals, dimension two. A normed division algebra, commutative and associative, but losing the order: they carry a square root of minus one, whose square is below zero, so no order can place them. This is the first rung past the integers, and the first loss in the tower, the loss of order.
form complex
  link source, like cayley-dickson-double
  link algebra, like normed-division-algebra

# The complex numbers, proof-checked by `seed test`.

# The norm is multiplicative for EVERY pair of complex numbers, the composition property that is the seed of the whole
# tower and of Hurwitz's theorem. With z = a + b i and w = c + d i, the product z w = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc) i, and the
# Brahmagupta-Fibonacci identity says (a*a + b*b) (c*c + d*d) = (ac - bd)^2 + (ad + bc)^2. A universal law over all
# a, b, c, d, proved by the commutative-ring normalizer.
rule complex-norm-is-multiplicative
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read a
          call multiply
            read b
            read b
        call add
          call multiply
            read c
            read c
          call multiply
            read d
            read d
      call add
        call multiply
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read a
              read c
            call multiply
              read b
              read d
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read a
              read c
            call multiply
              read b
              read d
        call multiply
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read d
            call multiply
              read b
              read c
          call add
            call multiply
              read a
              read d
            call multiply
              read b
              read c

# The Gaussian norm of 3 + 4i is the Pythagorean five: 3*3 + 4*4 = 25 = 5*5.
hold norm-of-three-plus-four-i-is-five
  call is-equal
    call add
      call multiply
        code 3
        code 3
      call multiply
        code 4
        code 4
    call multiply
      code 5
      code 5
